<BaseLayout title="About — Priyanga P Kini" description="Engineer at nilenso, working on distributed and analytical systems at scale.">
  <section class="intro intro-large">
    <h1 class="page-title">About <span class="title-accent">me</span></h1>
    <p>Hey, I’m Priyanga. I build software for a living, and I write here about what I learn doing it.</p>
    <p>I’m a member of <a href="https://nilenso.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link">nilenso</a>, a tech cooperative where we’re trying to build a new standard for tech consulting. That turns out to be as much about how you run a company as how you write software.</p>
    <p>I’ve been here five years now, working with our clients and sometimes on our own projects, mostly on distributed systems, data pipelines, and analytical systems.</p>
    <p>Right now I’m writing evals for <a href="https://github.com/dbreunig/botmap" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link">botmap</a>, a CLI for querying open map data that’s built to be driven by LLM agents rather than by people. Most of that comes down to working out how you tell whether an agent actually got the right answer.</p>
    <p>Before that I co-developed <a href="https://github.com/nilenso/megasthenes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link">Megasthenes</a>, an open-source TypeScript library that lets an AI agent take plain-English questions about any codebase and answer them with sourced, evidence-backed responses.</p>
    <p>At <a href="https://porter.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link">Porter</a>, the logistics company, I built a Temporal-based workflow for onboarding drivers, and moved a set of high-throughput APIs off a legacy Ruby Order Management System onto Kotlin services, which came out about 3x faster. I also built a document verification pipeline on Google Gemini that reads KYC documents at roughly 90% accuracy, for a tenth of what the previous solution cost.</p>
    <p>And before that, nearly three years at Resolve To Save Lives, on <a href="https://www.simple.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link">Simple</a>, open-source software that clinicians use to manage patients with hypertension and diabetes, across India, Bangladesh, Ethiopia, Sri Lanka and Myanmar. I built the backend behind its analytics dashboard, covering more than 6 million patient records, and migrated product workflows onto open health standards like DHIS2 and OpenSRP. A lot of the job was convincing myself the numbers were actually right. Auditing dashboards against 6M rows of SQL is less glamorous than it sounds, and more important.</p>
  </section>

